SC["a3c14_bridge"]={t:()=>SYS(`CHAPTER FOURTEEN — THE WANDERING LOOM`)+P(`Two days downriver, the boat puts in at a village with a problem it has decided to call a curse.`)+P(`The Whitestone Bridge — granite, six generations old, the only dry crossing for three valleys of market traffic — cracked its main span in the spring floods, and every mason who touches it swears the stone won't take mortar, the joins won't hold, the bridge doesn't <i>want</i> mending. The village has concluded, reasonably, that something is angry with them. Offerings rot on the bank. The ferryman, suddenly a monopolist, has tripled his fare and bought a new hat.`)+P(`You walk out on the broken span at dusk and look at it the way only you can. Nothing is angry. The flood didn't crack the bridge — it cut the binding-threads some forgotten formation-master wove through the stone six generations ago, and every mason since has been trying to mortar a wound that is actually an unraveling. The fix is an evening's quiet work for the one person alive who can see the thread-ends.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Mend it at midnight, unseen. Let the village wake to a miracle with no author.`,do:()=>{addIns(1);note("The reweave teaches you more than the trials did — six-generation-old thread, brittle as biscuit, accepting your gold like a graft. At dawn the village finds the crack sealed and gives loud thanks to a river god you've never met. He can have the credit. You keep the lesson.");},go:"a3c14_bride"},
  {id:"c1",l:`Mend it at noon, in front of everyone. Some curses deserve a public execution.`,do:()=>{G.f.a3_loud++;G.bao++;note("Gold light over the water at noon, a bridge knitting itself like a wound in reverse, and a hundred witnesses. By nightfall the story has a hero with golden hair; by next market it will have grown three extra spans and a dragon. Bao +1 — the version that reaches the mountain makes him cry laughing, says his letter.");},go:"a3c14_bride"}]};

SC["a3c14_bride"]={t:()=>P(`Third Reed Town, four days on, is mid-wedding and mid-disaster, the two being related.`)+P(`The bride is seventeen and has a face like a sealed jar you recognize from a different kitchen. The groom is the moneylender's son. The wedding contract, when you get a look at it — and you've been able to read contracts since a steward taught you what they cost — is a debt instrument wearing red silk: her family's flood-year loan, knotted to her name, payable in marriage or in ruin. Everyone in the courtyard knows it. The musicians play like men at a funeral with good acoustics.`)+P(`The basement taught you three ways to untie this kind of knot, and none of them is cutting the bride.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Re-knot the contract itself: find the clause where the interest compounds illegally, and read it aloud during the toasts.`,req:()=>(G.mind>=6),rq:"(needs Mind 6)",do:()=>{note("You stand up at the toasts with the scroll and the voice you learned from a discipline board. The moneylender's face goes through four seasons. The debt dies on its own terms — usury, witnessed — and the bride walks out of her own wedding through the front gate, free, while the musicians, sensing the room, strike up something with a pulse.");},go:"a3c14_shoal"},
  {id:"c1",l:`Pay it. The trials' purse, the stipend, whatever's in your sleeve — slap silver on the table like a verdict.`,req:()=>(G.f.a3_route=="sect"),do:()=>{G.heart++;note("+1 Heart. It's most of what you're carrying, and it buys exactly what silver should: a girl's name back. Her father tries to kneel; you don't let him. You eat at their table that night, and the soft-baked cakes taste like Tang's, which is to say, like a debt being paid forward.");},go:"a3c14_shoal"},
  {id:"c2",l:`Snare the groom's father mid-toast — let the courtyard watch the moneylender's own sleeve yank him face-first into the wedding cake. Then negotiate.`,do:()=>{G.f.a3_loud++;note("It is, you will admit later, not your subtlest work. It is extremely effective. The contract gets torn up by a man with frosting in his eyebrows and a sudden vivid sense of what the gold-haired stranger could have snared instead. The story doubles in size before you reach the dock.");},go:"a3c14_shoal"}]};

SC["a3c14_shoal"]={t:()=>P(`Halfway Shoal is where the river narrows between reed banks, and where, your boatman explains with resignation, the toll has recently been privatized.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Step onto the mudbank. You've been audited by professionals; this should be quaint.`,go:"a3c14_bandits"}]};

SC["a3c14_bandits"]={combat:"reedcutter",
 win:"a3c14_bandits_win",
 lose:"a3c14_bandits_lose",
 t:()=>""};

SC["a3c14_bandits_win"]={t:()=>P(`The Reed-Cutter sits down in the mud, what's left of his bracing sloughing off him like wet plaster, and looks up at you with the professional respect of one toll-collector for a superior operation. His crew, hidden in the reeds, audibly decide to be elsewhere.`)+P(`“You're him,” he says. “The bridge one. The wedding one.” He spits, thoughtfully. “Stories are getting around faster than you're traveling, friend.”`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Take his blade, leave him his boots, and ask what ELSE the stories say.`,do:()=>{addIns(1);note("Worth the asking: the stories aren't just spreading, they're being COLLECTED. Someone downriver has been buying every river-tale with a golden thread in it, paying in white silk thread, coin of no mint he knows.");},go:"a3c14_rumor"},
  {id:"c1",l:`Let him keep the blade — reeds need cutting — and the shoal needs an honest toll-man more than a beaten one.`,do:()=>{G.heart++;note("+1 Heart. He stares at you, then at the blade, then begins to laugh — the wheezing laugh of a man whose worldview has been mugged. The Halfway Shoal toll, travelers will report for years, is suddenly reasonable and includes tea.");},go:"a3c14_rumor"}]};

SC["a3c14_bandits_lose"]={t:()=>P(`The great blade catches you with the flat — deliberately, you realize later, the way professionals discourage rather than kill — and you wake in your own boat, boots gone, purse lighter, dignity untraceable.`)+P(`But the Reed-Cutter left your bundle, your sleeve of addresses, and a message pressed in mud on the gunwale, dictated to a literate crewman: <i>nothing personal. word is someone downriver pays white silk for tales of gold thread. you're worth more traveling than robbed. — R.C.</i>`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Worth more traveling. Someone is collecting you.`,do:()=>{addIns(1);G.hp=Math.min(G.maxhp,G.hp+5);note("+5 HP, eventually. The lesson lands harder than the blade did: the river has been reading you back.");},go:"a3c14_rumor"}]};

SC["a3c14_rumor"]={t:()=>P(`By the time the lake country's first pagodas show over the rushes, the rumor has finished being born, and you get to meet it in a wine-shop, told back to you by a drunk who has no idea he's talking to it.`)+((G.f.a3_loud>=1)?(P(`They call him the Knotless, the drunk explains, gripping your arm for emphasis. Hair like temple gold — seen by a hundred at Whitestone, no less. Mends what can't mend. Walks out of weddings with the bride's debt in his teeth. Fought the Reed-Cutter to a standstill with bare hands and unfair geometry. The drunk's version is loud, detailed, and accurate within a polite margin of error, and that is the problem: it travels with your face on it.`)):(P(`They call him the Knotless, the drunk explains, gripping your arm for emphasis — except nobody's seen him, see, that's the POINT. Bridges mend at midnight. Debts die of natural causes. A bandit retires to honest tolling and won't say why. The drunk's version is garbled, mystical, and three towns wrong about everything including your hair, which it insists is white, like a ghost's. You buy him another round on behalf of the ghost.`)))+P(`Either way, one detail repeats in every telling, and it isn't yours: somewhere in the lake country, a buyer with white silk thread for coin is collecting every story with gold in it. The stories are all flowing the same direction. Downriver. Where the receipts point.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Follow your own legend to Clearwater Hall.`,go:"a3c15_clearwater"}]};